</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/dba8d6a2-dc23-11f1-9698-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D6VU4CT.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/dba8d6a2-dc23-11f1-9698-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D6VU4CT.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5f2f5840-e52d-11f1-9b1a-a537c75f4a49</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:04:45 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/dba8d6a2-dc23-11f1-9698-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D6VU4CT.jpg">© Thomas Samson, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Guinea bans Burkina Faso broadcaster, escalating diplomatic tensions</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261120-guinea-bans-burkina-faso-broadcaster-escalating-diplomatic-tensions</link><description>
Tensions rise between Guinea and Burkina Faso after Conakry bans Burkinabe broadcaster RTB, amid a diplomatic dispute over gold miners and a caricature of President Mamadi Doumbouya. Meanwhile, fighting erupts between Congolese forces and the Rwanda-backed FRN group in eastern DR Congo, despite peace commitments. Finally, in Ethiopia, one entrepreneur is tackling period poverty by breaking menstrual stigma and making reusable sanitary pads more accessible.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8e31d6f6-e52b-11f1-9535-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a6bd8a5c.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8e31d6f6-e52b-11f1-9535-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a6bd8a5c.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4a97e566-e52b-11f1-9c5f-9fcf7db39ba3</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:53:35 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clémence WALLER</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>'Licence to kill': France adopts law presuming 'legitimate' self-defence in police shootings</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261120-licence-to-kill-french-parliament-adopts-police-shootings-law-slammed-activists</link><description>
French senators gave final approval on Friday to controversial legislation granting police officers a presumption of legitimate self-defence when using firearms, fulfilling a long-standing demand of the far right even as weeks of student-led protests have thrust the issue of police brutality back into the spotlight. Left-wing lawmakers have vowed to challenge the bill.
